<!-- fingertip_disk — a disk held at p1b fingertip height on a pedestal too heavy
     to push, so the hand can be made to close on something the fingertip contact
     sensors actually see.

     Loaded through rtc_mujoco_sim's object_pool, which attaches the body named
     `object` from `<directory>/<name>/object.xml` and adds its freejoint. So
     this file carries NO joint and NO scene — only the body.

     WHY THIS SHAPE. Every part of it answers a way the obvious object fails, and
     all five were observed in the p1b sim (#504, 2026-09-12) before this landed:

       * DISK, not a column. A column tall enough to reach the fingertips also
         occupies the volume the OPEN hand already fills, so it compiles inside
         the index finger or the palm before the fingers ever move — there is no
         (x,y) that works. Only the z band the fingertips alone sweep may be
         occupied, which in the shipped palm-down `initial_qpos` is roughly
         0.335..0.365 m. Hence r 0.019, h 0.03, and nothing else up there.
       * PEDESTAL AT 100 kg. A free object is pushed away rather than grasped: a
         0.5 kg column leaves the thumb in one step, and 5 kg still walks 2 cm in
         a single run. At 100 kg the table friction (mu 1 => ~1 kN) exceeds
         anything the hand can produce, so the run measures the HAND, not the
         object's inertia. The mass lives entirely in the pedestal; the neck and
         disk masses below are their density-derived values, spelled out so the
         total does not depend on a MuJoCo default.
       * NECK AT r 0.007. It only has to hold the disk up while staying thinner
         than the gap the closing fingers sweep, so the fingers reach the disk
         instead of stopping on the support.
       * PEDESTAL RADIUS 0.090 AND TOP AT z 0.30. Wide enough that the 100 kg is
         stable standing on the table, low enough to stay clear of the hand.

     GEOMETRY IS THE CONTRACT, and it is expressed relative to the BODY ORIGIN,
     which is what object_pool's `position` places. With the shipped spawn in
     `integrated_bringup/config/ur5e_p1b/sim_overlays/fingertip_grasp.yaml`
     (z = 0.051, a table top at z = 0.05), the three geoms below land at world
     z 0.05..0.30 (pedestal), 0.300..0.336 (neck) and 0.335..0.365 (disk). Move
     the spawn and the disk leaves the fingertip band — the overlay's z and this
     file's offsets are one number split in two places.

     PLACEMENT IN (x,y) IS NOT GUESSABLE. The fingertip links' contact radius is
     about 10 mm, not the 22 mm the published `l_*_tip_bracket_actual` frames
     suggest, and contacts on non-tip links carry no sensor at all. The shipped
     (x,y) came out of a `mujoco.mj_geomDistance` grid search over every robot
     collision geom; five eyeballed placements before it all missed or buried
     the disk. See `.claude/skills/verify/SKILL.md` (p1b) for the full checklist. -->
<mujoco model="fingertip_disk">
  <worldbody>
    <body name="object">
      <!-- Pedestal: bottom on the table top, i.e. 0.001 below the body origin. -->
      <geom name="fingertip_disk_pedestal" type="cylinder" size="0.090 0.125" pos="0 0 0.124"
            mass="100" contype="1" conaffinity="1" condim="4" friction="1 0.5 0.01"
            rgba="0.35 0.35 0.38 1"/>
      <!-- Neck: pedestal top up to the underside of the disk. -->
      <geom name="fingertip_disk_neck" type="cylinder" size="0.007 0.018" pos="0 0 0.267"
            mass="0.0055" contype="1" conaffinity="1" condim="4" friction="1 0.5 0.01"
            rgba="0.35 0.35 0.38 1"/>
      <!-- Disk: the only part the fingers are meant to touch. -->
      <geom name="fingertip_disk_disk" type="cylinder" size="0.019 0.015" pos="0 0 0.299"
            mass="0.034" contype="1" conaffinity="1" condim="4" friction="1 0.5 0.01"
            rgba="0.85 0.55 0.20 1"/>
    </body>
  </worldbody>
</mujoco>
